# RISC0 Image Handling Standards

## Context
- Applied when working with RISC-V program images in RISC0
- Focuses on safe loading and memory management
- Ensures proper validation of ELF files
- Critical for program execution safety
- Manages memory constraints and compression

## Requirements

### Image Structure
- Use proper image representation:
  ```rust
  pub struct Image {
      pub id: String,           // Unique identifier
      pub data: Option<Program>, // RISC-V program (optional for compression)
      bytes: Option<Bytes>,     // Raw bytes (optional for compression)
      pub size: u64,           // Size in bytes
      pub path: PathBuf,       // File path
      pub last_used: u64,      // Usage timestamp
  }
  ```

### Image Loading
- Implement safe ELF loading:
  ```rust
  fn load_elf(elf: &[u8]) -> Result<Program> {
      // Validate ELF format and memory constraints
      Program::load_elf(elf, GUEST_MAX_MEM as u32)?;
      Ok(program)
  }
  ```
- Validate memory constraints:
  ```rust
  const GUEST_MAX_MEM: usize = /* memory limit */;
  const PAGE_SIZE: usize = /* page size */;
  ```

### Memory Management
- Implement compression for memory efficiency:
  ```rust
  impl Image {
      // Free memory
      pub fn compress(&mut self) {
          self.data = None;
          self.bytes = None;
      }
      
      // Load back into memory
      pub async fn load(&mut self) -> Result<()> {
          if self.data.is_some() {
              return Ok(());
          }
          let data = read(&self.path).await?;
          let program = Image::load_elf(&data)?;
          self.data = Some(program);
          self.bytes = Some(Bytes::from(data));
          Ok(())
      }
  }
  ```

### Image Creation
- Support multiple creation methods:
  - From file path (async)
  - From bytes (for downloads)
  - With proper validation
- Generate unique IDs:
  - Based on memory image content
  - Using cryptographic hashing

## Examples

<example>
// Good: Complete image implementation with safety checks
impl Image {
    pub async fn new(path: PathBuf) -> Result<Image> {
        // 1. Load and validate file
        let data = read(&path).await?;
        
        // 2. Load and validate ELF
        let program = Self::load_elf(&data)?;
        
        // 3. Create and validate memory image
        let img = Self::mem_img(&program)?;
        
        // 4. Calculate size with overflow check
        let size = img.pages.len()
            .checked_mul(PAGE_SIZE as usize)
            .ok_or_else(|| anyhow::anyhow!("Size overflow"))?;
            
        Ok(Image {
            id: img.compute_id().to_string(),
            bytes: Some(Bytes::from(data)),
            data: Some(program),
            size: size as u64,
            path,
            last_used: 0,
        })
    }
    
    pub fn get_memory_image(&self) -> Result<MemoryImage> {
        // Validate program presence
        let program = self.data
            .as_ref()
            .ok_or_else(|| anyhow::anyhow!("No program data"))?;
            
        // Create memory image with validation
        let image = Self::mem_img(program)?;
        
        Ok(image)
    }
}
</example>

<example>
// Good: Memory-efficient image management
pub struct ImageManager {
    images: HashMap<String, Image>,
    max_memory: usize,
    current_memory: usize,
}

impl ImageManager {
    pub async fn load_image(&mut self, id: &str) -> Result<&Image> {
        let image = self.images.get_mut(id)
            .ok_or_else(|| anyhow::anyhow!("Image not found"))?;
            
        // Check memory limits
        if image.data.is_none() {
            let required = image.size as usize;
            if self.current_memory + required > self.max_memory {
                // Compress least recently used images
                self.free_memory(required)?;
            }
            
            // Load image
            image.load().await?;
            self.current_memory += required;
        }
        
        Ok(image)
    }
    
    fn free_memory(&mut self, required: usize) -> Result<()> {
        let mut freed = 0;
        let mut to_compress = Vec::new();
        
        // Find least recently used images
        for (_, image) in self.images.iter() {
            if image.data.is_some() {
                to_compress.push((image.last_used, image.id.clone()));
            }
        }
        
        // Sort by last used
        to_compress.sort_by_key(|(ts, _)| *ts);
        
        // Compress until enough memory is freed
        for (_, id) in to_compress {
            if let Some(image) = self.images.get_mut(&id) {
                freed += image.size as usize;
                image.compress();
                if freed >= required {
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
        
        if freed < required {
            return Err(anyhow::anyhow!("Cannot free enough memory"));
        }
        
        self.current_memory -= freed;
        Ok(())
    }
}
</example>

